"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 4.3 and the pathological T2 stage observed post-prostatectomy. Positive prognostic indicators include negative surgical margins, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no documented lymph node metastasis. The Gleason score of 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) is noted from the prostatectomy specimen. A countervailing finding is that the biopsy source provided a missing Gleason pattern and ISUP report, creating an informational gap compared to the definitive surgical pathology findings.\n\nThe absence of nodal status documentation in the summary does not apply here as no nodes were reported removed or sampled for metastasis. The postoperative PSA trajectory remains unknown. Future information regarding serial postoperative PSA measurements would be critical for refining risk assessment. Given the current data, the prognosis is based on the favorable local and regional pathology findings documented at surgery. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 67.81–101.71 months; its coverage has not been validated."
